br0w+@andrew.cmu.edu (Bruno W. Repetto) writes:
>[B]  - Zombies should be the ones to turn undead, not trolls.

There has been some confusion on rec.games.hack as to the proper usage of the
term "turn undead."  In general fantasy-role-playing usage, the term refers
to the ability of a religious persona or magical item to turn away undead,
cause them to run (as a vampire will cringe from a holy symbol - which might 
be an interesting item to add to the game, incidentally) or possibly even
be destroyed if the priest in question is powerful enough.

Now, in nethack, a wand of "turn undead" has the side effect of being able
to re-animate corpses (in addition to the normal turn undead ability).  This
reanimation is different from that produced by regeneration (as in trolls).

hairston@henry.ece.cmu.edu (David Hairston) writes:
>hmm, i wasn't quite clear on this point.  if an elf dies, i think there
>should be a chance that (somehow) its corpse re-animates as an elf zombie!,
>and so on for other potential zombie types ...

Basically, of the undead types, skeletons and zombies are the two lowest
orders.  This is the case because skeletons and zombies are not naturally
ocurring - they must be animated by a powerful mage or priest.  More 
powerful undead creatures exist due to their powerful tie to the material
plane - even after they die and go to the negative plane, they still 
maintain a tie (and thus some form of existence) in the prime material 
plane.

Perhaps we could add Revenants to nethack - the Wizard would be a prime
example of a Revenant.  Basically, a creature with a powerful enough will
would be able to will itself to return to the prime material plane for the
sole purpose of seeking out and destroying the person(s) who was responsible
for its death.  (See AD&D Fiend Folio, p.75 for more information.)  This
is limited to intelligent creatures - but can you imagine that shopkeeper
that you killed getting lucky and coming back to haunt you?  FUN!

